    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "While there is significant evidence that Comprehensive Sexuality Education (CSE) yields\r\npositive outcomes, there is a lack of access to CSE for children and adolescents around the\r\nworld. The purpose of this study is to examine the current landscape of sexual education (SE) in\r\nBrazil and compare Brazil’s national policies for school-based SE with the United Nations\r\nEducational, Scientific, and Cultural Organization’s (UNESCO) guidelines for CSE.\r\nSpecifically, I analyse Brazil’s national public policies, national curriculum, and teacher\r\neducation. Through qualitative document analysis, this research highlights the misalignment\r\nbetween Brazilian national policies and UNESCO’s guidelines, the influence of local progressive\r\nmovements, and the impact of conservative waves. The study emphasizes the importance of\r\ndeveloping and implementing clear and inclusive public policies on CSE, with age-appropriate\r\ninformation for all students, empowerment of educators, and multi-sectoral collaboration beyond\r\nthe school environment.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ruiz de Oliveira, N. (2023). Sexual Education in Brazil: A Comparison of National Policies with UNESCO’s Guidelines. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/vc335xp1148. https://doi.org/10.25740/vc335xp1148.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
